definition:
    French chemist and physicist (1897–1956), daughter of Marie and Pierre Curie and, with her husband Frédéric, discoverer of artificial radioactivity in 1934 — earning the 1935 Nobel Prize in Chemistry as only the second woman ever to win one. She served as Undersecretary of State for Scientific Research in post-war France and helped shape early French reactors. Like her mother, she died of a radiation-linked blood disease (leukemia), a consequence of working before protection standards existed.

debunking_snippet:
    “Radiation safety rules are bureaucratic overkill.” They were written in the blood of pioneers like Irène Joliot-Curie and her mother — brilliant scientists who handled sources bare-handed because nobody knew better yet.
